So you have to show your skills in spoken English and general
presentation. Then the content of the talk seems pretty clear: Show the
audience your fascination for the game and try to explain whence it
comes. 

Are you allowed to play the game? If not, it might get hard. Some ideas:
Maybe I would start to talk about a game which is in development for more
than 20 years (Not NetHack in itself, but let's be generous.), and which
has an inconceivable attention to details. Then I would go over some of
the eulogies on NetHack available on the Internet. This will make you
sound like a geek, but when you present a talk on a computer game,
there's no way around it anyway.

Then, when everyone is exited on what is about to come, show them a
simple text mode screen of an in-game scene. Watch your classmates drop
their jaws.

Mention that the game has a very persistent following. Some people play
for years before they win, which is hardly the case for any other
computer game. Mention the iron mode gaming. Talk about the vital
community (newsgroup, spoiler writers, variants). Emphasise the NetHack
is not just another computer game; that it has, instead, a very special
appeal, which makes it different from most other games, and nearly all
comercial ones.

If your teacher is fond of this, you might show some of the entries in
the data base file from famous English writing authors (cobra, flesh
golem, dagger etc.).


Of course, usual tips on a good talk apply: Don't squeeze to many lines
on one slide, use colours sparsely and consistently, don't try fancy
overblending effects in a laptop based presentation, give the audience a
thread to follow, provide context information etc.

